{"title":"2026 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Problems/Problem 6","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Quadrilateral <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/7/9a7dc02d05e6f5ab9e48c7f281d8642f3452fead.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$RSTU$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"56\" height=\"14\" /> is formed by placing two equilateral triangles together, as shown.\n</p>\n<hr />\n<p><i>An image is supposed to go here.  You can help us out by creating one and <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=2026_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit\">editing it in</a></span>.  Thanks.</i>\n</p>\n<hr />\n<p>The measure of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/b/e/8beb8ef068e7cee307ccac72452e57e5ab49076d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle RST$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"54\" height=\"14\" /> is\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/d/79db848135f1820a459aff809499261a69947127.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}\\ 60^{\\circ} \\qquad\\textbf{(B)}\\ 90^{\\circ} \\qquad\\textbf{(C)}\\ 100^{\\circ} \\qquad\\textbf{(D)}\\ 120^{\\circ} \\qquad\\textbf{(E)}\\ 150^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"462\" height=\"19\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Each interior angle in any <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Equilateral_triangle\" title=\"Equilateral triangle\">equilateral triangle</a> has a measure of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/8/d/08d5c45388de5c75af641c046d466de3592ffa98.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$60^{\\circ}$\" width=\"24\" height=\"13\" />. This means that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/a/6/5a6b3ba4d19a2fa05a162614fcb17a7c76b28464.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle RSU$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"55\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/5/0/1502716f52339683a8c348b57f349382abdde889.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle TSU$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"54\" height=\"14\" /> each have a measure of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/8/d/08d5c45388de5c75af641c046d466de3592ffa98.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$60^{\\circ}$\" width=\"24\" height=\"13\" />. \n</p><p>Their measures also sum up to the measure of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/b/e/8beb8ef068e7cee307ccac72452e57e5ab49076d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle RST$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"54\" height=\"14\" />, so the measure of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/b/e/8beb8ef068e7cee307ccac72452e57e5ab49076d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle RST$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"54\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/3/a/13abacb93af5b215c7830e9b13be870de9d1400c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$60^{\\circ} \\times 2 = \\boxed{\\textbf {(D) } 120^{\\circ}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"165\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p><p>~anabel.disher\n</p>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)\" title=\"2026 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8)\">2026 CEMC